> Basically, this just means that you try to buy as much 
> protection from a priest as you can.  One way is the 
> protection dive, which consists of avoiding (almost) 
> any level gain while collecting money, then diving 
> (hence the name) down to the minetown priest as 
> possible.  Views upon the value of this strategy are 
> controversial; while some consider the gain of natural 
> AC 1 - (-10) early in game well worth the risk, others 
> strongly disagree, pointing out that even with very clever 
> playing, one is all too easily separated from his/her pet 
> and in consequence easily doomed while diving in the mines.  

Of course, this depends to some extent what is your ultimate goal in the 
game.  I think it could be easily proven that the protection racket 
diminishes the rate of characters that make it, say, to the castle 
(except *possibly* for healers).  On the other hand, if the goal is not 
to maximize survival rate but just fun, then one can easily argue (and 
this would be difficult to counter) that the protection racket is well 
worth the risk, for while only a few characters will survive it, they 
may have a nicer time afterwards.  Sacrifice many to get along smoothly 
with the few surviving ones...
